                <pb facs="0099" n="9" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg9"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.00/1629: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Long</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-03-30T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 30,
                                1920—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>59. Your 66, March 16, 6 p.m.<anchor n="14" xml:id="d9-f1"/></p>
                <p>The Department is apprehensive lest the approval of amendments by President
                    Menocal lead to further movement for amendment electoral law. You are therefore
                    directed to observe with greatest care whether any further movement in this
                    direction is apparent. The Department maintains unchanged its attitude with
                    reference to the undesirability of the amendments as passed and is particularly
                    anxious to discourage any further tinkering with the electoral law. You are
                    therefore authorized if, in your opinion, it will contribute toward this end,
                    orally to state to President Menocal that the Department was surprised at the
                    undue haste which he showed in approving the amendments, especially as he was
                    aware of the Department’s attitude …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Colby</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
